.TH SORT 1 "September 2026" "mitos-utils 0.1.0" "MITOS User Commands"
.SH NAME
sort \- sort lines of text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sort
[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-u\fR] [\fB\-k\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-t\fR \fIDELIM\fR] [\fIFILE\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sort the concatenated lines of FILE(s) (or standard input) and write the result to standard output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the sort order.
.TP
.B \-n
Compare according to numeric value.
.TP
.B \-u
Output only the first of each run of equal lines.
.TP
.B \-k \fIN\fR
Sort by field N (1-indexed) instead of the whole line. A single field only, not an \fIN\fR,\fIM\fR range.
.TP
.B \-t \fIDELIM\fR
Field delimiter for \fB\-k\fR (default: any whitespace run).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Every mitos-utils command additionally accepts \fB\-\-help\fR (print a usage line and exit) and \fB\-\-version\fR. Commands that take file/path arguments also accept \fB\-\-\fR to mark the end of options, so a file literally named e.g. \fB\-oddfile\fR can still be referenced.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a general failure (a listed file could not be processed, etc.), 2 on a usage error (missing or invalid arguments).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mitos\-box (1),
.BR mitos\-utils\-compat (7),
.BR mitos\-utils\-integration (7)
